---
import ConverterPage from '../components/ConverterPage.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is this for every Chanakya font?',
    a: 'No. It writes code for one font, <strong>Abbasi Chanakya New</strong> (Jamal Abbasi, 2010). Other Chanakya fonts, such as Walkman Chanakya 905, put some letters on different keys, so this output will show the wrong letters in them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'The output looks like random English letters. Is that a bug?',
    a: 'No, that is what Abbasi Chanakya New text is. It only becomes Hindi once the font is applied to it. Paste it into Word, select it and set the font to <strong>Abbasi Chanakya New</strong>, or use the Download .docx button, which applies the font for you. Press <strong>Preview (Hindi)</strong> above the output to see it in the font here.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does it say Beta?',
    a: 'The mapping was built from the font file and every key was checked by eye, but it has not yet been compared with a real document typed in this font. Converting the output back with the Abbasi Chanakya to Unicode page returns your original text, but please check anything important.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Some characters were left as-is. Why?',
    a: 'This font has no key for them, for example ॐ or ऌ. They are left in the output unchanged and a note appears under the box. Hindi digits (०-९) become 0-9, because this font draws its digits in Latin form. A visarga (ः) becomes the : key, which looks the same in this font.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens to quotation marks?',
    a: 'Straight quotes become curly ones. In this font the <code>\'</code> and <code>"</code> keys are the letters श् and ष्, so a straight quote cannot be written as itself.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens to English words and numbers?',
    a: 'Numbers, dates, URLs and email addresses are left alone, the same as on the Unicode to Kruti Dev page. You can switch this off with the checkbox under the boxes.',
  },
];
---

<ConverterPage
  path="/unicode-to-abbasi-chanakya"
  lede="Type or paste Hindi on the left and get Abbasi Chanakya New font code on the right. Hindi typed on a phone or Windows keypad is already Unicode, so it works here directly."
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>What this converter does</h2>
  <p>
    Abbasi Chanakya New is a legacy Hindi font: it draws Hindi letters on
    ordinary keyboard characters. To use Unicode Hindi in a document set in that
    font, the text has to be turned into those characters and put back into the
    order the font types them in. Every short-i matra moves to the front of its
    consonant cluster (<strong lang="hi">हिन्दी</strong> becomes
    <code>fgUnh</code>), and every reph moves to the end of its syllable
    (<strong lang="hi">कर्म</strong> becomes <code>deZ</code>).
  </p>

  <h2>What to do with the output</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Press <strong>Copy Abbasi Chanakya code</strong>, or use
      <strong>Download .docx</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>Paste into Word or your DTP application.</li>
    <li>
      Select the pasted text and set the font to
      <strong>Abbasi Chanakya New</strong>. The .docx download does this for you.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    To go the other way, use the
    <a href="/abbasi-chanakya-to-unicode">Abbasi Chanakya to Unicode converter</a>.
    If you need Kruti Dev instead, use
    <a href="/unicode-to-kruti-dev">Unicode to Kruti Dev</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A note on exact spellings</h2>
  <p>
    Some letters can be typed more than one way in this font (ध, for example,
    has three keys). The converter always picks one, so its output may not match
    text a person typed by hand character for character. It looks the same in
    the font, and converting it back returns the text you started with.
  </p>
</ConverterPage>
